WN.chunk(14,{"n":[[481,"Classify objects into given categories, count the number in each category, and sort the categories by count",["Sort a set of shapes by colour and count how many in each group","Sort objects by size (big/small) and state how many in each category","Identify which category has the most/fewest after sorting and counting"],["ccss-math:K.MD.3"]],[482,"Interpret and construct simple pictograms, tally charts, block diagrams, and simple tables",["Read a pictogram where each symbol represents one item","Construct a tally chart from collected data","Draw a block diagram to represent data from a survey"],["uk-nc-2013:Ma/KS2/Y3/S/1","uk-nc-2013:Maths/Y2/S/1"]],[483,"Read, write, and use the vocabulary of data collection and display — data, tally, tally chart, frequency, frequency table, survey, pictogram, bar chart, axis/axes, scale, label, category, discrete data, continuous data, line graph, pie chart — and apply these terms when collecting, organising, and presenting data",["Correctly label the axes of a bar chart including a title, axis labels, and scale","Distinguish between discrete data (counted) and continuous data (measured) with an example of each","Use 'tally', 'frequency', and 'pictogram' correctly when describing how to record and display data"],[]],[484,"Organise and represent data with up to three categories by counting objects in each category and sorting categories by quantity",["Sort a set of objects into 2-3 given categories and count each group","Create a simple table or list showing category names and counts","Order categories from most to fewest or fewest to most"],["ccss-math:1.MD.4","uk-nc-2013:Ma/KS2/Y3/S/2","uk-nc-2013:Maths/Y2/S/2"]],[485,"Interpret categorical data by asking and answering questions about totals, how many in each category, and how many more or less one category has than another",["Answer 'how many?' for each category in a data set","Calculate the total number of data points across all categories","Compare two categories using 'how many more/fewer' language"],["ccss-math:1.MD.4","uk-nc-2013:Ma/KS2/Y3/S/3","uk-nc-2013:Maths/Y2/S/3"]],[486,"Draw a picture graph and a bar graph (with single-unit scale) to represent a data set with up to four categories; solve put-together, take-apart, and compare problems using information presented in a bar graph",["Draw a picture graph with a symbol representing one unit for each data point","Draw a bar graph with labelled axes and a single-unit scale","Use a bar graph to answer comparison questions (e.g. 'How many more votes did cats get than dogs?')"],["ccss-math:2.MD.10"]],[487,"Interpret and present discrete and continuous data using appropriate graphical methods, including bar charts and time graphs",["Read a time graph showing temperature changes over a day","Present data about plant growth over weeks as a time graph","Explain the difference between a bar chart (discrete) and a time graph (continuous)"],[]],[488,"Draw a scaled picture graph and a scaled bar graph to represent a data set; solve one- and two-step comparison, sum, and difference problems using bar charts, pictograms, and tables",["Draw a bar graph where each square represents 5 pets","From a scaled pictogram, answer: how many more children chose football than tennis?","Solve a two-step problem: how many votes in total for the top two choices?"],[]],[489,"Solve comparison, sum, and difference problems using information presented in a bar graph",["Read a line graph of temperature over a week and identify the warmest day","Find the difference in rainfall between two months from a line graph","Explain what a rising/falling line means on a time-series graph"],[]],[490,"Complete, read, and interpret information in tables, including timetables",["Read a bus timetable to find the departure time for a particular stop","Complete a two-way table from given data about favourite sports by gender","Calculate how long a train journey takes using a timetable"],[]],[491,"Read, write, and use the vocabulary of statistical analysis — mean, median, mode, range, frequency, data, sample, average, chart, table, graph, pie chart, scatter graph, correlation — with understanding of what each term describes",["Correctly define and calculate the mean, median, mode, and range of a small data set","Use 'outlier' correctly to identify a value that doesn't fit the pattern, and explain its effect on the mean","Use 'correlation' correctly when describing the relationship shown in a scatter graph"],[]],[492,"Calculate and interpret the mean as an average of a data set",["Calculate the mean of five test scores: 72, 85, 90, 68, 95","Explain what the mean represents and how it differs from individual data values","Find a missing data value given the mean and all other values"],[]],[493,"Interpret and construct pie charts and line graphs; use these to solve problems",["Read values from a line graph showing temperature over a day and identify trends","Construct a pie chart from given data by calculating sector angles","Use a pie chart to determine the actual quantity represented by each sector given the total"],[]],[494,"Describe, interpret, and compare distributions of a single variable using appropriate measures of central tendency (mean, median, mode) and spread (range), including the effect of outliers",["Calculate mean, median, and mode for a data set and explain when each is most appropriate","Find the range of a data set and explain how an outlier affects the mean versus the median","Compare two data sets using their averages and ranges to draw conclusions"],["ccss-math:7.SP.3","ccss-math:7.SP.4","uk-nc-2013:KS3.Maths.Stat.1"]],[495,"Display numerical data on dot plots and equal-width histograms, and read off features such as clusters, gaps and peaks",["Draws a dot plot of a small data set","Groups data into equal classes and draws a histogram","Describes clusters and gaps"],["ccss-math:6.SP.4","ccss-math:S-ID.1"]],[496,"Calculate and interpret the mean absolute deviation as the average distance of data values from the mean",["Calculates the MAD of a small data set","Compares variability of two data sets using MAD","Explains what a larger MAD means"],["ccss-math:6.SP.5c"]],[497,"Construct and interpret frequency tables, bar charts, pie charts, pictograms, and vertical line charts for both categorical and grouped numerical data, choosing appropriate representations for the data type",["Construct a grouped frequency table from raw continuous data, choosing appropriate class intervals","Draw a pie chart by calculating the angle for each category","Interpret a bar chart comparing two data sets and draw a conclusion about the difference"],["ccss-math:7.SP.1","ccss-math:7.SP.2","uk-nc-2013:KS3.Maths.Stat.2"]],[498,"Recognise a statistical question as one that expects variability in the answers (e.g. 'How tall are students in my class?') and understand that a data set has a distribution described by centre, spread and shape",["Sorts questions into statistical and non-statistical","Explains why 'How old am I?' is not a statistical question","Describes a data set using centre and spread"],["ccss-math:6.SP.1","ccss-math:6.SP.2"]],[499,"Classify data as categorical or numerical, and numerical data as discrete or continuous, and choose suitable displays for each type",["Classifies shoe size, height and eye colour","Explains why height is continuous","Chooses a suitable chart for each type"],["uk-nc-2013:KS3.Maths.Stat.1"]],[500,"Calculate the mean, median and mode from ungrouped frequency tables",["Calculates the mean from a frequency table","Finds the median position in a frequency table","Checks the answer is plausible"],["uk-nc-2013:KS3.Maths.Stat.1"]],[501,"Choose the most appropriate average for a data set and context, explaining how outliers and skew affect the mean more than the median",["Explains why median is better for house prices","Shows how one outlier changes the mean","Justifies a choice of average in context"],["ccss-math:6.SP.5d"]],[502,"Distinguish a population from a sample, explain why samples are used, and understand that a sample is only valid for a population if it is representative",["Identifies the population and sample in a study","Explains why a census is not always possible","Explains what 'representative' means"],["ccss-math:7.SP.1"]],[503,"Identify sources of bias such as convenience samples, voluntary response, leading questions and non-response, and suggest improvements",["Explains why surveying a gym about exercise habits is biased","Rewrites a leading question","Suggests how to reduce non-response"],["ccss-math:7.SP.1"]],[504,"Draw and interpret box plots from the five-number summary, reading off median, quartiles and range",["Draws a box plot from a five-number summary","Reads the median and IQR from a box plot","Explains what the length of the box represents"],["ccss-math:6.SP.4","ccss-math:S-ID.1"]],[505,"Plan a data collection: write clear unbiased questions with suitable response options, choose a sampling method, and pilot the questionnaire",["Writes a question with non-overlapping response boxes","Chooses and justifies a sampling method","Identifies a problem from a pilot"],[]],[506,"Estimate the mean from grouped frequency tables using midpoints, identify the modal class and the class containing the median, and explain why the mean is an estimate",["Uses midpoints to estimate the mean","Identifies the modal class","Explains why the answer is only an estimate"],["uk-nc-2013:KS3.Maths.Stat.1"]],[507,"Use data from a random sample to estimate population characteristics, such as scaling a sample proportion up to a population",["Estimates the number in a population from a sample proportion","Explains why the estimate may be wrong","Compares estimates from different samples"],["ccss-math:7.SP.2"]],[508,"Find the lower quartile, median, upper quartile and interquartile range of a data set, and explain why IQR is resistant to outliers",["Finds the quartiles of an ordered list","Calculates the IQR","Explains why IQR is better than range when there are outliers"],["ccss-math:6.SP.5c"]],[509,"Describe and carry out simple random, systematic and stratified sampling, and explain why random selection helps produce representative samples",["Describes how to choose a simple random sample","Calculates numbers for a stratified sample","Compares methods for a given situation"],["ccss-math:7.SP.1"]],[510,"Describe simple mathematical relationships between two variables using scatter graphs, identify positive, negative, or no correlation, and use a line of best fit to make predictions",["Plot bivariate data on a scatter graph and describe the type of correlation observed","Draw a line of best fit by eye and use it to estimate a value within the data range","Explain what positive, negative, and no correlation mean in the context of real data (e.g., temperature vs. ice-cream sales)"],["ccss-math:8.SP.1","ccss-math:8.SP.2","ccss-math:8.SP.3","uk-nc-2013:KS3.Maths.Stat.3"]],[511,"Identify ways graphs and statistics mislead — truncated axes, unequal intervals, 3-D effects, cherry-picked data, inappropriate averages — and redraw a fair version",["Identifies a truncated axis","Explains how a pictogram can exaggerate","Redraws a misleading graph fairly"],["ccss-math:S-IC.6"]],[512,"Plot and interpret time series graphs, describing trends and seasonal patterns and making cautious predictions",["Plots a time series","Describes trend and seasonal variation","Explains why predictions far ahead are unreliable"],[]],[513,"Construct and interpret two-way tables of categorical data, calculating joint, marginal and conditional relative frequencies to look for associations",["Completes a two-way table from information","Calculates row and column relative frequencies","Describes whether there is an association"],["ccss-math:8.SP.4","ccss-math:S-ID.5"]],[514,"Use the capture–recapture method to estimate the size of an animal population, stating the assumptions it relies on",["Calculates a population estimate from capture data","States assumptions of the method","Explains how violating an assumption changes the estimate"],[]],[515,"Compare two distributions using box plots, commenting on centre (median) and spread (IQR) in context",["Writes a comparison of medians in context","Writes a comparison of IQRs in context","Draws a conclusion supported by both"],["ccss-math:S-ID.2"]],[516,"Draw cumulative frequency graphs from grouped data and use them to estimate the median, quartiles and the number above or below a value",["Plots cumulative frequency at upper class boundaries","Estimates median and IQR from the graph","Estimates how many values exceed a given value"],[]],[517,"Explain that different random samples give different results, that this variation shrinks as sample size grows, and generate multiple samples to see it",["Simulates several samples and compares their means","Explains why larger samples give more consistent estimates","Judges whether a difference could be due to chance"],["ccss-math:7.SP.2","ccss-math:S-IC.1"]],[518,"Describe distributions as symmetric, positively or negatively skewed, or bimodal, and explain how shape affects the relative positions of mean and median",["Identifies skew from a histogram","Predicts whether mean or median is larger for skewed data","Explains a bimodal shape in context"],["ccss-math:S-ID.3"]],[519,"Draw and interpret histograms with unequal class widths using frequency density = frequency ÷ class width, and estimate frequencies from areas",["Calculates frequency densities","Draws a histogram with unequal widths","Estimates a frequency from part of a bar"],[]],[520,"Use the 1.5 × IQR rule to identify outliers, show them on box plots, and decide whether to keep or remove them in context",["Calculates outlier fences","Marks outliers on a box plot","Argues whether an outlier is an error or genuine"],["ccss-math:S-ID.3"]],[521,"Interpret the correlation coefficient r as a measure of the strength and direction of a linear association between −1 and 1, recognising that r can be near zero for a strong nonlinear pattern and is affected by outliers",["Matches scatter plots to values of r","Explains what r = −0.9 means in context","Explains why r ≈ 0 does not mean no relationship"],["ccss-math:S-ID.8"]],[522,"Calculate (with technology) and interpret the standard deviation as a typical distance from the mean, and compare it with IQR and MAD",["Calculates standard deviation with a calculator","Compares spread of two sets using standard deviation","Explains when IQR is preferable"],["ccss-math:S-ID.2"]],[523,"Distinguish sample surveys, observational studies and randomised experiments, and explain that only randomised experiments can establish cause and effect",["Classifies studies by type","Explains the role of random assignment","Explains why an observational study cannot prove causation"],["ccss-math:S-IC.3"]],[524,"Explain why a binomial critical region usually has a probability below the nominal significance level, and calculate this actual significance level",["Calculates the probability of the critical region under H₀","Explains why it differs from 5%","Links the actual level to the chance of a wrong rejection"],[]],[525,"Use data from a randomised experiment to compare two treatments, using simulation to decide whether a difference is larger than chance would produce",["Calculates the difference in means between groups","Uses re-randomisation to see what chance produces","Concludes whether the difference is significant"],["ccss-math:S-IC.5"]],[526,"Find the critical region for a test (the values of the test statistic that lead to rejecting H₀) and decide the outcome by checking whether the observed value lies in it",["Finds a critical region for a one-tailed binomial test","Identifies the critical value","Uses the critical region to reach a decision"],[]],[527,"Use sample survey data to estimate a population mean or proportion, and develop a margin of error through simulation",["Estimates a population proportion from a sample","Uses simulation to find a likely range of results","Interprets a margin of error in a news report"],["ccss-math:S-IC.4"]],[528,"Evaluate reports and news stories that use data, checking study type, sample, measures used, graphs and whether conclusions are justified",["Identifies the study type behind a headline","Spots an unjustified causal claim","Writes a critique of a data-based article"],["ccss-math:S-IC.6"]],[529,"Fit quadratic and exponential functions to data with technology, compare them with a linear fit using residuals, and choose the most appropriate model",["Fits an exponential model to population data","Compares residual plots of two models","Justifies a choice of model in context"],["ccss-math:S-ID.6.a","ccss-math:S-ID.6.b"]],[530,"Carry out a complete hypothesis test for a binomial proportion: hypotheses, null distribution, p-value or critical region, decision and conclusion in context",["Carries out a one-tailed test","Carries out a two-tailed test","States a conclusion in context without claiming proof"],[]],[531,"Use calculators, spreadsheets or tables to find areas under the normal curve and so estimate population percentages",["Finds the proportion above a value","Finds the proportion between two values","Finds a value given a percentage"],["ccss-math:S-ID.4"]],[532,"Write null and alternative hypotheses about a population parameter (e.g. H₀: p = 0.25, H₁: p > 0.25), distinguishing parameters from sample statistics",["Writes H₀ and H₁ for a context","Explains why hypotheses use p and not the sample proportion","Chooses the direction of H₁ from the claim"],[]],[533,"Choose a one-tailed or two-tailed test from the wording of the claim before seeing data, and split the significance level between both tails for a two-tailed test",["Decides between one and two tails from a claim","Splits 5% into 2.5% in each tail","Explains why the choice must be made before looking at data"],[]],[534,"Use a randomisation (re-allocation) simulation to judge whether a difference between two treatment groups is significant, and estimate a p-value from the simulation",["Explains why group labels are shuffled","Estimates a p-value from a simulated distribution","Decides whether a difference is significant"],["ccss-math:S-IC.5"]],[535,"Explain the significance level as the threshold probability for rejecting the null hypothesis (often 5% or 1%), chosen before collecting data, and explain the effect of choosing a smaller level",["Explains what a 5% significance level means","Explains why the level is set in advance","Explains the trade-off of using 1% instead of 5%"],[]],[536,"Calculate z-scores to compare values from different distributions and to locate values on a normal curve",["Calculates a z-score","Compares performances on two different tests","Interprets a negative z-score"],["ccss-math:S-ID.4"]],[537,"Identify a suitable test statistic (such as the number of successes) and its distribution if the null hypothesis is true",["States the test statistic for a context","Writes its distribution under H₀, e.g. X ~ B(20, 0.5)","Explains why the distribution assumes H₀"],[]],[538,"Decide whether a probability model is consistent with observed results, for example by simulating outcomes from a fair coin and comparing",["Simulates outcomes from a model","Compares an observed result with simulated results","Decides whether the model should be questioned"],["ccss-math:S-IC.2"]],[539,"Use technology to find the least-squares regression line, explain that it minimises the sum of squared residuals, and interpret its gradient and intercept in context",["Finds a regression line with technology","Explains what 'least squares' means","Interprets the gradient in context"],["ccss-math:S-ID.6.a","ccss-math:S-ID.6.c","ccss-math:S-ID.7"]],[540,"Explain the logic of a hypothesis test: assume a model (the null hypothesis), ask how surprising the data would be under it, and treat a very surprising result as evidence against the model, never as proof",["Explains why the null model is assumed true during the test","Explains what makes a result 'surprising'","Explains why a test cannot prove a hypothesis"],["ccss-math:S-IC.2"]],[541,"Recognise normally distributed data as symmetric and bell-shaped, described by mean and standard deviation, and use the 68–95–99.7 rule to estimate proportions",["Sketches a normal curve with mean and standard deviation marked","Uses the 68–95–99.7 rule","Recognises data sets for which a normal model is not appropriate"],["ccss-math:S-ID.4"]],[542,"Use x̄ to estimate μ and s² = Σ(x − x̄)²/(n − 1) to estimate σ², explaining why dividing by n − 1 removes the bias of dividing by n",["Calculates s² from summary statistics","Explains why dividing by n underestimates σ² on average","Chooses the correct calculator output for an estimate"],[]],[543,"Write conclusions that refer to the context, the evidence and the significance level ('there is sufficient evidence at the 5% level to suggest…'), avoiding claims that H₀ has been proved or accepted",["Writes a conclusion in context","Corrects a conclusion that says 'this proves'","Explains the difference between 'not rejecting' and 'accepting' H₀"],[]],[544,"Calculate a p-value as the probability, assuming H₀, of a result at least as extreme as the one observed, compare it with the significance level, and avoid common misinterpretations",["Calculates a p-value from a binomial distribution","Compares a p-value with the significance level","Explains why a p-value is not the probability that H₀ is true"],[]],[545,"Check the assumptions behind t procedures (random independent samples, approximate normality and, for a pooled test, similar variances) using plots and summary statistics, and judge how robust a conclusion is",["Checks a sample for skew and outliers before using t","Explains why a large sample relaxes the normality condition","Explains when a pooled test is inappropriate"],[]],[546,"Test whether observed frequencies fit a proposed distribution (such as a fair die or a stated ratio) by comparing observed and expected frequencies with a chi-squared statistic",["Calculates expected frequencies from a model","Combines classes when expected frequencies are small","Concludes whether the model fits"],[]],[547,"Test whether two categorical variables are associated using a chi-squared test on a contingency table, calculating expected frequencies and comparing the statistic with a critical value",["Calculates expected frequencies","Calculates the chi-squared statistic and degrees of freedom","Concludes about association in context"],[]],[548,"Choose a suitable test (binomial, normal mean, proportion, correlation or chi-squared) for a question and data type, and check the test's conditions",["Matches questions to tests","Checks conditions before testing","Explains why a test is not appropriate for given data"],[]],[549,"Decide whether data are paired or come from independent samples, and explain how pairing reduces variability and changes the test",["Classifies designs as paired or independent","Explains why analysing paired data as independent loses power","Chooses the correct test for a given design"],[]],[550,"Test for a difference between two population means using z = (x̄ − ȳ)/√(σ₁²/n₁ + σ₂²/n₂) when the variances are known or the samples are large",["Explains where the standard error of the difference comes from","Calculates the test statistic","Concludes in context"],[]],[551,"Construct and interpret a confidence interval for a population mean as x̄ ± z·σ/√n, explaining what the confidence level means and how the interval relates to a two-tailed test",["Calculates a 95% confidence interval","Interprets the interval correctly","Explains how a larger sample changes the width"],["ccss-math:S-IC.4"]],[552,"Construct and interpret a confidence interval x̄ ± t·s/√n for a population mean when σ is unknown, and explain why it is wider than the corresponding z-interval",["Calculates a 95% t-interval","Interprets the interval in context","Explains why small samples give wider intervals"],["ccss-math:S-IC.4"]],[553,"Explain degrees of freedom as the number of values free to vary once constraints are fixed, why a t-test on n values uses n − 1, and how a contingency table gives (r − 1)(c − 1)",["Explains why n deviations from x̄ have n − 1 degrees of freedom","Finds degrees of freedom for t and chi-squared tests","Describes how degrees of freedom change the shape of a distribution"],[]],[554,"Test the fit of a binomial or Poisson model to frequency data, estimating a parameter from the data when needed and reducing the degrees of freedom by one for each estimated parameter",["Calculates expected frequencies from a Poisson model","Combines classes so that expected frequencies are at least 5","Reduces the degrees of freedom for an estimated parameter"],[]],[555,"Test a claim about a Poisson rate with H₀: λ = λ₀, rescaling λ₀ for the observation period and using a critical region or p-value from the Poisson distribution",["Writes hypotheses about a rate in context","Finds a critical region for a one-tailed test","Concludes in context from an observed count"],[]],[556,"Test a claim about a population mean using z = (x̄ − μ₀)/(σ/√n), with critical values or p-values, for one- and two-tailed tests",["Calculates the test statistic","Uses critical values such as 1.645 and 1.96","Concludes in context"],[]],[557,"Use the individual (O − E)²/E contributions in a contingency table to describe which cells drive a significant association and in which direction",["Identifies the cells with the largest contributions","Compares observed and expected values in those cells","Writes a conclusion describing the nature of the association"],[]],[558,"Test a claim about a population proportion with a large sample, using a normal approximation for the sample proportion and checking that the conditions hold",["Checks large-sample conditions","Calculates a z statistic for a proportion","Compares the result with an exact binomial test"],[]],[559,"Explain why running many tests at 5% will produce some 'significant' results by chance, recognise p-hacking and selective reporting, and explain the role of replication",["Estimates how many false positives to expect from 20 tests","Explains what p-hacking is","Explains why replication matters"],[]],[560,"Test a claim about the mean of a normal population with unknown variance using t = (x̄ − μ₀)/(s/√n) with n − 1 degrees of freedom, for one- and two-tailed tests",["Calculates the t statistic","Finds the critical value from tables or technology","Concludes in context"],[]],[561,"Analyse paired data (such as before-and-after measurements on the same people) by testing the mean of the differences with a one-sample t-test",["Calculates the differences and their mean and standard deviation","Writes hypotheses about the mean difference","Concludes in context"],[]],[562,"Explain power as the probability of rejecting H₀ when it is false (1 − P(type II error)), and describe how sample size, significance level and effect size affect it",["Defines power","Calculates P(type II error) for a given alternative","Explains why larger samples increase power"],[]],[563,"Distinguish a statistically significant result from a practically important one, explaining how very large samples can make tiny effects significant",["Explains why a tiny difference can be significant with a huge sample","Uses effect size as well as p-value","Evaluates a headline based on a significant result"],[]],[564,"Test whether a population correlation coefficient ρ is zero using the sample value r and a table of critical values for the sample size and significance level",["Writes hypotheses about ρ","Finds the critical value for given n and significance level","Concludes in context and avoids claiming causation"],[]],[565,"Explain that sample means vary less than individual values, that X̄ ~ N(μ, σ²/n) for normal populations (approximately for large n), and interpret σ/√n as the standard error",["Explains why sample means vary less than individual values","Calculates the standard error","Uses the distribution of X̄ to find a probability"],[]],[566,"Explain that when σ is estimated by s from a small sample of a normal population, (x̄ − μ)/(s/√n) follows a t-distribution with n − 1 degrees of freedom, which has heavier tails than the normal and approaches it as n grows",["Explains why replacing σ by s changes the distribution","Compares t critical values with 1.96","Describes how the t-distribution changes with degrees of freedom"],[]],[567,"Compare the means of two independent groups with a two-sample t-test, using a pooled variance when the population variances can be assumed equal, and interpret the result for an experiment",["Calculates a pooled estimate of variance","Calculates the t statistic with n₁ + n₂ − 2 degrees of freedom","Concludes whether the treatments differ"],["ccss-math:S-IC.5"]],[568,"Define type I errors (rejecting a true H₀) and type II errors (failing to reject a false H₀), find the probability of a type I error, and explain the trade-off between them",["Defines both errors in context","States that P(type I error) equals the actual significance level","Explains how reducing one error tends to increase the other"],[]],[569,"Calculate the probability of a type I error (the actual significance level) and of a type II error for a given alternative value of λ in a Poisson test",["Finds the actual significance level of a Poisson test","Finds P(type II error) when λ takes a stated value","Explains how the errors trade off"],[]]],"r":[[63,"Cross-subject: interpreting categorical data by asking/answering questions relies on the English skill of asking relevant questions to seek information"],[64,"Interpreting data requires having data organised and represented first"],[231,"Describing correlation and using a scatter graph requires 'correlation', 'scatter graph', and 'line of best fit'"],[232,"Scatter graphs require confident coordinate plotting in the first quadrant and beyond"],[233,"Line of best fit connects to understanding linear relationships from y = mx + c"],[234,"Interpreting scatter graphs requires understanding averages and data distributions"],[381,"Drawing accurate pie chart sectors requires protractor skills"],[382,"Y6 line graphs extend Y5 line graph interpretation"],[383,"Constructing pie charts requires knowing angles at a point sum to 360°"],[384,"Pie chart sector angles connect to percentage calculation"],[385,"Constructing pie charts and line graphs requires the display vocabulary"],[418,"Constructing pictograms, tally charts, and bar charts requires these display vocabulary terms"],[419,"Constructing pictograms and tally charts requires classifying and counting objects first"],[420,"Data representation formats (pictograms, tally charts) support organising data"],[446,"Calculating the mean requires 'mean' and 'average' vocabulary"],[447,"Calculating mean requires decimal addition"],[448,"Calculating mean requires division giving decimal answers"],[449,"Scaled bar charts are prerequisite to continuous data and time graphs"],[450,"Distinguishing discrete from continuous data and choosing graphical methods requires these terms"],[467,"Time conversion supports interpreting timetables"],[468,"Reading scaled charts/tables is prerequisite to interpreting timetables"],[570,"KS3 chart construction extends KS2 pie charts and line graphs to grouped data"],[571,"Frequency tables extend KS2 table interpretation to grouped numerical data"],[572,"Constructing and interpreting frequency tables and charts draws on frequency and distribution vocabulary"],[573,"Pie chart construction requires calculating percentages/fractions for angle calculation"],[717,"Y4 bar charts and time graphs are prerequisite to line graph problem-solving"],[1314,"Constructing simple pictograms/tables is prerequisite to scaled versions"],[1315,"Drawing scaled bar charts and pictograms requires axis, scale, label, and frequency vocabulary"],[1316,"Single-unit bar graphs are prerequisite to scaled bar graphs"],[1549,"Drawing picture/bar graphs extends organising and representing data"],[1682,"Sorting categories by count benefits from ability to compare quantities"],[1683,"Counting objects in each category requires being able to count sets of objects"],[1691,"KS3 averages and spread extend KS2 mean calculation to include median, mode, and range"],[1692,"Describing and comparing statistical distributions requires constructing and reading frequency tables, bar charts, and pie charts"],[1693,"Describing distributions using mean, median, mode, and range requires all these statistical terms"],[1746,"Counting data in categories requires understanding cardinality"],[1747,"Organising and representing data requires data, tally, frequency, and category vocabulary"],[1748,"Organising data in categories builds on classifying and counting objects in categories"],[2708,"Words such as data, variable and distribution are used to describe what a question asks"],[2709,"Collecting and tallying answers shows that a statistical question gives varied answers"],[2710,"Classifying data as categorical, discrete or continuous uses the vocabulary of statistics"],[2711,"Which chart suits the data, such as a bar chart or histogram, depends on the type of data"],[2712,"Frequency tables made from tallies supply the counts plotted on dot plots and histograms"],[2713,"Histograms are used for numerical data that is continuous or grouped into classes"],[2714,"The mean of a list is extended to a frequency table by multiplying each value by its frequency"],[2715,"The data arrive as frequency tables, which must be read correctly before averaging"],[2716,"The frequency-table mean is extended to grouped data by using each class midpoint"],[2717,"Comparing data sets by their typical values leads to choosing the most suitable average"],[2718,"Seeing how an outlier drags the mean needs fluent calculation of the mean"],[2719,"Each deviation is the distance of a value from the mean, so the mean is found first"],[2720,"Absolute deviation is the distance between two numbers on a number line, ignoring direction"],[2721,"The median and range used to compare data sets are refined into quartiles and the IQR"],[2722,"A box plot draws the five-number summary of minimum, quartiles, median and maximum"],[2723,"Comparing two distributions starts with reading the median and IQR from each box plot"],[2724,"Comparisons are written about both a typical value and how spread out the data are"],[2725,"The median and quartiles are read from the curve at half and quarter of the total frequency"],[2726,"The curve is plotted from a grouped frequency table using upper class boundaries"],[2727,"Equal-width histograms are extended to unequal widths, where area shows frequency"],[2728,"The data come in grouped frequency tables with class widths to read off"],[2729,"Frequency density is a rate, frequency per unit of class width"],[2730,"Skew and symmetry are read from the shape of a dot plot or histogram"],[2731,"Shape explains why the mean is pulled towards the tail more than the median"],[2732,"The 1.5 × IQR rule uses the quartiles, and outliers are marked separately on a box plot"],[2733,"Deciding whether a value is unusual depends on the overall shape of the distribution"],[2734,"Standard deviation measures typical distance from the mean, as MAD does, but with squares"],[2735,"The calculation squares each deviation and then takes a square root"],[2736,"Reading values from rows and columns is needed before finding joint and marginal totals"],[2737,"Relative frequencies express one quantity as a fraction of another"],[2738,"A time series is a line graph of a quantity against time, read for trend and seasonality"],[2739,"Reading bar charts and pictograms carefully is needed to spot misleading scales or symbols"],[2740,"Truncated axes and uneven time intervals often appear on line graphs"],[2741,"Describing correlation from a scatter graph as strong or weak becomes a number r"],[2742,"r measures how closely the points follow a straight line, so the idea of a line of fit is needed"],[2743,"The least-squares line formalises the line of best fit drawn by eye"],[2744,"The line is the one that makes the sum of squared residuals as small as possible"],[2745,"r is reported with the regression line to show how reliable predictions from it are"],[2746,"Linear, quadratic and exponential fits are compared by their residual plots"],[2747,"An exponential fit uses the a·bˣ growth model and its parameters"],[2748,"A quadratic fit uses the quadratic model and its turning point"],[2749,"A sample is taken to answer a statistical question about a larger population"],[2750,"Each method chooses a sample from a clearly defined population, such as all students in a school"],[2751,"Random selection means every member of the population has an equal probability of being chosen"],[2752,"A stratified sample splits the sample size in the same ratio as the groups in the population"],[2753,"Bias means the sample does not represent the population, so the distinction is needed"],[2754,"Clear questions and good sampling are chosen to avoid leading questions and non-response"],[2755,"The plan names a random, systematic or stratified sampling method"],[2756,"Planning a survey builds on writing a clear, testable question for an investigation"],[2757,"Scaling a sample result up to the population is only justified if the sample was chosen randomly"],[2758,"A sample proportion is scaled to the population by direct proportion"],[2759,"Capture–recapture assumes the marked proportion in the second sample matches the population"],[2760,"The estimate N = (first catch × second catch) ÷ marked comes from setting two proportions equal"],[2761,"Different random samples give different estimates, which limits how far one estimate can be trusted"],[2762,"Simulating many random samples shows how much the results vary and how this shrinks with size"],[2763,"Each study type is judged partly by how well it avoids biased samples and responses"],[2764,"Observational studies show association only, while randomised experiments can show cause"],[2765,"Experiments control other variables and assign treatments randomly, unlike observational studies"],[2766,"A margin of error puts a size on the sample-to-sample variation of an estimate"],[2767,"The margin is found by simulating many samples and seeing how far their estimates spread"],[2768,"Spread of sample results is measured with standard deviation"],[2769,"Only a randomised experiment lets a difference between treatments be put down to the treatment"],[2770,"Re-randomising the group labels by simulation shows how big a difference chance alone produces"],[2771,"Many simulated runs of the model, such as a fair coin, show which results are typical"],[2772,"Observed counts are compared with the frequencies the model predicts"],[2773,"Whether a report can claim a cause depends on whether it describes an experiment or an observation"],[2774,"Graphs in reports are checked for truncated axes and other misleading features"],[2775,"A reported difference smaller than the margin of error may be due to sampling alone"],[2776,"A normal curve is described by its mean and standard deviation, as in the 68–95–99.7 rule"],[2777,"The normal distribution is a particular symmetric, bell-shaped distribution with no skew"],[2778,"z-scores measure distance from the mean in standard deviations"],[2779,"Values are converted to z-scores before areas are found from tables or technology"],[2780,"Areas between two values are found by subtracting, and the total area is 1, as with probabilities"],[3041,"Asking whether data are surprising under a model formalises checking whether a model fits"],[3042,"Whether a result is surprising is judged against normal sample-to-sample variation"],[3043,"H₀ states the model assumed true, and H₁ the change being looked for"],[3044,"Hypotheses are about a population parameter such as p, not the sample result"],[3045,"The significance level sets how unlikely a result must be under H₀ to count as surprising"],[3046,"The null distribution of the test statistic comes from assuming H₀ is true"],[3047,"The first null distributions met are binomial, so the conditions for a binomial must hold"],[3048,"The p-value is a tail probability calculated from the null distribution"],[3049,"H₀ is rejected when the p-value is below the significance level"],[3050,"A binomial p-value such as P(X ≥ 9) is a cumulative probability"],[3051,"The critical region is the set of test-statistic values that would lead to rejecting H₀"],[3052,"The critical region is chosen so its probability under H₀ is at most the significance level"],[3053,"Binomial critical values are found by checking cumulative probabilities against the level"],[3054,"The actual significance level is the probability under H₀ of landing in the critical region"],[3055,"Whether H₁ says 'greater', 'less' or 'different' decides one or two tails"],[3056,"In a two-tailed test, the significance level is split equally between the two tails"],[3057,"The decision can be made by comparing a binomial p-value with the significance level"],[3058,"The decision can instead be made by checking whether the result lies in the critical region"],[3059,"The wording of the claim decides whether the binomial test is one- or two-tailed"],[3060,"A conclusion states in context what the decision of the test means"],[3061,"Careful wording avoids the overstated claims criticised when judging reports"],[3062,"The probability of a type I error equals the actual significance level of the test"],[3063,"Type I and type II errors are the two ways a test's decision can be wrong"],[3064,"Power is 1 − P(type II error), the chance of correctly rejecting a false H₀"],[3065,"Sample means of a normal population follow a normal distribution, N(μ, σ²/n)"],[3066,"The variance σ²/n puts a number on how sample means vary less with larger samples"],[3067,"The standard error σ/√n is a standard deviation of the sample mean"],[3068,"The test statistic uses the distribution of x̄ under H₀, with standard error σ/√n"],[3069,"x̄ is standardised to z = (x̄ − μ₀)/(σ/√n) before comparing with critical values"],[3070,"The claim decides whether the critical value is one-tailed or two-tailed"],[3071,"The decision uses a p-value or critical value, as in the binomial test"],[3072,"The sample count is approximated by a normal distribution when n is large"],[3073,"A large-sample test for p extends the binomial test, where exact calculation becomes awkward"],[3074,"The z-statistic and decision follow the same steps as the test for a normal mean"],[3075,"The interval x̄ ± z·σ/√n is built from the standard error of the sample mean"],[3076,"z·σ/√n is the margin of error, now calculated from a formula rather than by simulation"],[3077,"The test asks whether a sample value of r is strong enough to suggest ρ ≠ 0"],[3078,"Testing for positive, negative or any correlation decides one or two tails"],[3079,"r is compared with a critical value from a table for the sample size"],[3080,"A significant correlation still does not show that one variable causes the other"],[3081,"Comparing treatment groups in a randomised experiment is the setting for the test"],[3082,"The group labels are re-shuffled many times by simulation to see what chance alone gives"],[3083,"The p-value is estimated as the proportion of shuffles at least as extreme as the result"],[3084,"The observed counts are arranged in a two-way contingency table"],[3085,"Expected counts assume independence, using P(A and B) = P(A) × P(B)"],[3086,"The chi-squared statistic is compared with a critical value for its degrees of freedom"],[3087,"Expected frequencies under the proposed distribution are probability × total count"],[3088,"The statistic is compared with a chi-squared critical value at the chosen level"],[3089,"The statistic Σ(O − E)²/E is the same one used in the test for independence"],[3090,"The binomial test is the choice for a count of successes in a fixed number of trials"],[3091,"The normal mean test is the choice for a claim about a mean with known σ"],[3092,"The correlation test is the choice for a question about association in paired data"],[3093,"The chi-squared test is the choice for association between two categorical variables"],[3094,"A tiny p-value shows strong evidence of an effect but says nothing about its size"],[3095,"Asking whether an effect matters in practice is part of judging reports"],[3096,"Very large samples have high power, so they can make tiny effects significant"],[3097,"Each test at 5% has a 5% chance of a type I error, so many tests produce false positives"],[3098,"Judging research claims includes asking how many tests were run before one was significant"],[3303,"Tail probabilities under H₀ are found from cumulative Poisson values"],[3304,"The decision can be made by finding the critical region for the Poisson count"],[3305,"The decision can instead be made by comparing a p-value with the significance level"],[3306,"The null rate λ₀ is rescaled to the length of the observation period"],[3307,"The steps of the test follow the same structure as a binomial test"],[3308,"The error probabilities are calculated from the Poisson test's critical region"],[3309,"The definitions of type I and type II errors are applied to a Poisson test"],[3310,"The type I error probability is the actual significance level"],[3311,"The variance of a data set is adjusted to estimate the population variance"],[3312,"Estimating μ and σ² means telling sample statistics from population parameters"],[3313,"Estimates vary from sample to sample, and unbiased ones are right on average"],[3314,"The n − 1 divisor in s² is the first example of losing one degree of freedom"],[3315,"Generalises the degrees of freedom used in chi-squared tests"],[3316,"The t-statistic replaces σ by s in the standardised sample mean"],[3317,"s, the unbiased estimate of σ, is what makes the distribution t rather than normal"],[3318,"Each t-distribution is indexed by its degrees of freedom, n − 1 for one sample"],[3319,"The t-distribution is compared with the normal curve, having heavier tails"],[3320,"The null distribution of the test statistic is a t-distribution with n − 1 degrees of freedom"],[3321,"The t-test follows the z-test for a mean, with s replacing σ"],[3322,"The wording of the claim decides whether the t-test is one- or two-tailed"],[3323,"The interval uses a t critical value with n − 1 degrees of freedom"],[3324,"The z-interval x̄ ± z·σ/√n is adapted by replacing z with t and σ with s"],[3325,"The paired test is a one-sample t-test applied to the list of differences"],[3326,"Recognising a matched-pairs design, such as before and after, decides the method"],[3327,"Pairing removes variation between subjects when comparing two treatments"],[3328,"The difference of two independent sample means is normal with added variances"],[3329,"The test follows the structure of the z-test for a single mean"],[3330,"The two-sample test extends the one-sample t-test to compare two groups"],[3331,"The variance of x̄ − ȳ is the sum of the two variances, even though the means are subtracted"],[3332,"The z-test for two means is the model when variances are known"],[3333,"A randomisation test compares the same two groups by simulation"],[3334,"The checks are for the conditions behind the t-test already used"],[3335,"Outliers strongly affect s, so they are checked before using a t procedure"],[3336,"Approximate normality is judged from the shape of a dot plot or histogram"],[3337,"The paired test, on differences within each pair, is one of the two methods compared"],[3338,"The two-sample test is the other method, for independent groups"],[3339,"Choosing between paired and two-sample tests extends choosing a test for the data"],[3340,"The goodness-of-fit test is extended to binomial and Poisson models"],[3341,"Expected frequencies for a Poisson model are probabilities times the total"],[3342,"One degree of freedom is lost for each parameter estimated from the data"],[3343,"Expected frequencies for a binomial model are binomial probabilities times the total"],[3344,"The overall statistic is broken down into the (O − E)²/E contribution from each cell"],[3345,"The largest contributions are described in context in the test's conclusion"],[4115,"Sorting into categories builds on matching things that are the same"],[4116,"Sorting into categories extends sorting by colour, size and shape"]],"c":{"Mathematics|Data & Statistics|5":"Your child is learning to organise and count information by sorting objects into groups and comparing which groups have more or fewer items.","Mathematics|Data & Statistics|6":"Your child is learning to work with data — creating and reading simple charts, graphs, and tables to organise information and answer questions about what the data shows.","Mathematics|Data & Statistics|7":"Your child is learning to create and read simple graphs — making picture graphs and bar charts to show information, then using these graphs to solve mathematical problems.","Mathematics|Data & Statistics|8":"Your child is learning to collect information and present it visually using charts and graphs, then use these displays to solve problems and answer questions about the data.","Mathematics|Data & Statistics|9":"Your child is learning to work with data — reading timetables and graphs, and solving problems by interpreting the information presented in charts and line graphs.","Mathematics|Data & Statistics|10":"Your child is learning to interpret and create different types of graphs and charts, and beginning to calculate averages to understand what data tells us about real-world situations.","Mathematics|Data & Statistics|11":"Your child is learning what statistics is for — asking questions that have variable answers, sorting data into types, drawing dot plots and histograms, and measuring spread with the mean absolute deviation.","Mathematics|Data & Statistics|12":"Your child is learning to summarise data — finding averages from frequency tables, choosing the most suitable average when there are outliers, and understanding the difference between a population and a sample.","Mathematics|Data & Statistics|13":"Your child is learning to collect and summarise data well — using quartiles, box plots and two-way tables, choosing random samples and spotting bias, and recognising misleading graphs and statistics.","Mathematics|Data & Statistics|14":"Your child is learning to compare sets of data — using box plots and cumulative frequency graphs, describing the shape of a distribution, and understanding why results vary from one sample to another.","Mathematics|Data & Statistics|15":"Your child is learning to measure and question data — calculating standard deviation, spotting outliers, reading histograms with unequal bars, interpreting correlation, and telling surveys, experiments and observational studies apart.","Mathematics|Data & Statistics|16":"Your child is learning how statisticians draw conclusions — fitting regression lines, using the normal distribution, and following the logic of a hypothesis test with hypotheses, significance levels, p-values and critical regions.","Mathematics|Data & Statistics|17":"Your child is learning advanced statistical testing — t-tests and confidence intervals, chi-squared tests, tests for correlation and for Poisson rates, the risk of wrong conclusions, and how to choose the right test for the data."}});
